  <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <div type="mūlam" rend="parallel">
      <lg xml:id="TNV6">
      <l>teyva māṉiṭañ ceyti yuṇavō</l>
<l>ṭeytiya vicaivilaṅ kiṉaiyaṉa piṟavum</l>
<l>poytīr karuveṉap pukaṉṟaṉar koḷalē.<anchor xml:id="kolale"/></l></lg>
<lg xml:lang="en">
  <l>The gods, the people, the occupations, the food,</l> 
  <l>the suitable music, the animals,
        and other such things</l>
        <l> – [let there be an] understanding<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">Alternatively, one can translate this as a kind of polite command – ‘Please understand…’. In other places, similar forms are glossed by commentators using an optative form (see Cēṉāvaraiyar's <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">itaṉ porul</foreign> for TC 296c and 297c; see also Nacciṉārkkiṉiyar's <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">itaṉ porul</foreign> on the same <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cūttiram</foreign>-s, numbered 297 and 298 in his count, for example).</note> that they have said that [these] are
        the true<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poy tīr</hi></q> is a kind of formula.
        Literally it means: ‘x in which falsity has ended’. In practice, this means that something
        is true.</note> constituent elements.</l></lg>
</div>
</body>
</text>
<text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <head xml:lang="en">Commentary</head>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
      <p xml:id="TNV6comm1">eṉpatu karup poruḷ ām āṟuṇarttutaṉutaliṟṟu.</p>
      <p xml:lang="en">The quoted [<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>] intends to make known the way in which the
        poetic matter of the constituent elements occurs.</p></div>
        <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
      <p xml:id="TNV6comm2">collap paṭṭa mutaṟ poruṭ kuriya teyvamum vaḻi<anchor xml:id="vali"/> vāḻumakkaḷum 
      avarkaḷ toḻilkaḷum uṇpārkkut tuppākiya vuṇavum icaiyum āṅkaṇ vāḻum vilaṅkukaḷum 
      piṟavum ain tiṇaikkup peṟṟa karup poruḷ ām eṉṟa vāṟu. <num>6.</num></p>
      <p xml:lang="en">The things suitable for the poetic matter in the aforementioned basic
        elements – the gods, the people who exist on the paths,<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">In the sense of ‘in those places’ or ‘there’.</note> their occupations, the food that
        is the nourishment for those who eat it, the music, the animals that live there, and other
        things – are the poetic matter of the constituent elements related to the five poetic
        settings.</p>
        </div>
      </body>
      </text>
      <standOff type="notes4" corresp="#TNV6comm2">
        <note xml:lang="en" target="#vali">This can also be read as <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">teyvamum avaḻi</hi></q>, which in itself makes no sense, but UVC may have thought the scribe had simply missed the gemination that comes along with the deictic prefix <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">a-</hi></term>, or miscopied and left out one <hi rend="italic">v</hi>, and then made the minor emendation to “<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">avvaḻi</hi></term>”. The chosen reading – <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">vaḻi</hi></q> – retains a similar sense and is found in C1.</note>
      </standOff>
